#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082950 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2684fbd5-5ae7-50ec-bb0e-05dc8c55df80" :knowledge-point/title "Factoring a Sum of Squares"}] :question/id #uuid "0bc42213-4681-59fb-9390-e56740cf1f1e" :question/math-academy-id "e-5784" :question/problem "Factor $m^2 + 16n^2$." :question/worked-solution "To factor a sum of squares, we first write it as a difference. Then, we rewrite the second term using\n\n$$\n\\textrm{i}^2 = -1\n$$\n\nFinally, we factor as a difference of squares.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm^{2} + 16n^{2} &= m^{2} - (-16n^{2}) \\\\\n&= m^{2} - (16\\textrm{i}^{2}n^{2}) \\\\\n&= m^{2} - (4^{2}\\textrm{i}^{2}n^{2}) \\\\\n&= m^{2} - (4\\textrm{i}n)^{2} \\\\\n&= (m + 4\\textrm{i}n)(m - 4\\textrm{i}n)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082951 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8216c637-fe4d-51fd-b256-0379eb0e7dba" :knowledge-point/title "Factoring a Sum of Higher-Degree Squares"}] :question/id #uuid "6b17455c-780d-5843-b9cb-3d1a8abdb4ac" :question/math-academy-id "e-5785" :question/problem "Factor $25x^4 + 4 y^8$." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given expression is a sum of squares, since\n\n$$\n25x^4 + 4 y^8 = \\left(5x^2\\right)^2 + \\left(2 y^4\\right)^2\n$$\n\nTo factor a sum of squares, we first write it as a difference. Then, we rewrite the second term using\n\n$$\n\\textrm{i}^2 = -1\n$$\n\nFinally, we factor as a difference of squares.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n25x^{4} + 4y^{8} &= 25x^{4} - (-4y^{8}) \\\\\n&= 25x^{4} - (4\\textrm{i}^{2}y^{8}) \\\\\n&= (5x^{2})^{2} - (2\\textrm{i}y^{4})^{2} \\\\\n&= (5x^{2} + 2\\textrm{i}y^{4})(5x^{2} - 2\\textrm{i}y^{4})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082952 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2de6dbad-0ca5-5a17-a04b-cdfd72d6dab5" :knowledge-point/title "Factoring an Expression Containing a Sum of Squares"}] :question/id #uuid "7d37eaaf-02aa-5a0b-b0a3-32b3c5b7ec35" :question/math-academy-id "e-5786" :question/problem "Factor $-z^3-25z$." :question/worked-solution "First, let's factor $-z$ from the expression. We have\n\n$$\n-z^3-25z =-z(z^2+25)\n$$\n\nLet's now focus on factoring the expression\n\n$$\n\\left(z^2+25\\right)\n$$\n\nTo factor a sum of squares, we first write it as a difference. Then, we rewrite the second term using\n\n$$\n\\textrm{i}^2 = -1\n$$\n\nFinally, we factor as a difference of squares.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nz^{2} + 25 &= z^{2} - (-25) \\\\\n&= z^{2} - (25\\textrm{i}^{2}) \\\\\n&= z^{2} - (5\\textrm{i})^{2} \\\\\n&= (z + 5\\textrm{i})(z - 5\\textrm{i})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting this back into our main expression, we get\n\n$$\n-z(z^2+25)=-z(z+5\\textrm i)(z-5\\textrm i)\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082953 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c55fcfa4-0050-5a80-a142-29616155797f" :knowledge-point/title "Factoring a Perfect Square Quartic Trinomial"}] :question/id #uuid "2df8c4e2-04ec-5b14-904f-cbc8dcf1d35e" :question/math-academy-id "e-5787" :question/problem "Factor $x^4+18x^2+81$." :question/worked-solution "First, let's factor the expression as a perfect square trinomial in $x^2$. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{4} + 18x^{2} + 81 &= (x^{2})^{2} + 2 \\cdot 9 \\cdot (x^{2}) + (9)^{2} \\\\\n&= (x^{2} + 9)^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to factor the sum of squares in the parentheses. To do this, we first write it as a difference. Then, we rewrite the second term using\n\n$$\n\\textrm{i}^2 = -1\n$$\n\nFinally, we factor as a difference of squares.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} + 9 &= x^{2} - (-9) \\\\\n&= x^{2} - (9\\textrm{i}^{2}) \\\\\n&= x^{2} - (3\\textrm{i})^{2} \\\\\n&= (x + 3\\textrm{i})(x - 3\\textrm{i})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting this back into our main expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{4} + 18x^{2} + 81 &= (x^{2} + 9)^{2} \\\\\n&= ((x + 3\\textrm{i})(x - 3\\textrm{i}))^{2} \\\\\n&= (x + 3\\textrm{i})^{2}(x - 3\\textrm{i})^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262186 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8216c637-fe4d-51fd-b256-0379eb0e7dba" :knowledge-point/title "Factoring a Sum of Higher-Degree Squares"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "52dd5c12-cb2f-5a9c-9b95-3df7f5012747" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(7{t}^{6}+3\\text{i})(7{t}^{6}-3\\text{i})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262188} {:answer/id #uuid "04141cc7-73a3-57e7-b905-0884ea8e2fbd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9(7{t}^{6}+\\text{i})(7{t}^{6}-\\text{i})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262189} {:answer/id #uuid "633fa9e4-b7c4-514e-b80f-ee59cf61c55f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "49({t}^{6}+3)({t}^{6}-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262190} {:answer/id #uuid "38a82961-a357-5232-8064-4432bc46e66e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(7{t}^{6}+3)(7{t}^{6}-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262191} {:answer/id #uuid "37637c55-4a7f-59e9-a30b-b1cf5ceca5ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "49({t}^{6}+3\\text{i})({t}^{6}-3\\text{i})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262192}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "52dd5c12-cb2f-5a9c-9b95-3df7f5012747" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(7{t}^{6}+3\\text{i})(7{t}^{6}-3\\text{i})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262188} :answer-field/id #uuid "f3c72336-8067-5cc1-aa03-7a61729d441d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262187}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c96cb0b5-0ea7-5886-9424-b0c6b79e9292" :question/math-academy-id "q-170499" :question/problem "$49{t}^{12}+9=$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given expression is a sum of squares, since\n\n$$\n49{t}^{12}+9={(7{t}^{6})}^{2}+{3}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTo factor a sum of squares, we first write it as a difference. Then, we rewrite the second term using ${\\text{i}}^{2}=-1.$ Finally, we factor as a difference of squares.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}49{t}^{12}+9 & =49{t}^{12}-(-9) \\\\  & =49{t}^{12}-(9{\\text{i}}^{2}) \\\\  & ={(7{t}^{6})}^{2}-(3\\text{i})^{2} \\\\  & =(7{t}^{6}+3\\text{i})(7{t}^{6}-3\\text{i})\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262193 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c55fcfa4-0050-5a80-a142-29616155797f" :knowledge-point/title "Factoring a Perfect Square Quartic Trinomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c26b584a-58cd-5c24-af0b-00b6578c622b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9(t+\\sqrt{5})^{2}(t-\\sqrt{5})^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262195} {:answer/id #uuid "16ee580c-25f9-59f9-9050-40166c47d38e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3t+5\\text{i})^{2}(3t-5\\text{i})^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262196} {:answer/id #uuid "5d84227a-3f31-552c-9b98-eba9e55e2c45" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\sqrt{3}t+\\sqrt{5})^{2}(\\sqrt{3}t-\\sqrt{5})^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262197} {:answer/id #uuid "f3db476d-337a-5742-986b-07e3d6c742b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\sqrt{3}t+\\sqrt{5}\\text{i})^{2}(\\sqrt{3}t-\\sqrt{5}\\text{i})^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262198} {:answer/id #uuid "28836f53-20b3-5352-9dba-70756d09472e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\sqrt{3}t+\\sqrt{5}\\text{i})^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262199}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f3db476d-337a-5742-986b-07e3d6c742b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\sqrt{3}t+\\sqrt{5}\\text{i})^{2}(\\sqrt{3}t-\\sqrt{5}\\text{i})^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262198} :answer-field/id #uuid "3b8d6efa-3171-55f1-848f-4a2c01829e74" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262194}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "21e2d89f-0660-5a0d-981b-df2f4731097d" :question/math-academy-id "q-170501" :question/problem "Factor $9{t}^{4}+30{t}^{2}+25.$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's factor the expression as a perfect square trinomial in ${t}^{2}.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{t}^{4}+30{t}^{2}+25 & ={(3{t}^{2})}^{2}+2\\cdot (3{t}^{2})\\cdot 5+{5}^{2} \\\\  & =(3{t}^{2}+5)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to factor the sum of squares in the parentheses. To do this, we first write it as a difference. Then, we rewrite the second term using ${\\text{i}}^{2}=-1.$ Finally, we factor as a difference of squares.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{t}^{2}+5 & =3{t}^{2}-(-5) \\\\  & =3{t}^{2}-(5{\\text{i}}^{2}) \\\\  & =(\\sqrt{3}t)^{2}-(\\sqrt{5}\\text{i})^{2} \\\\  & =(\\sqrt{3}t+\\sqrt{5}\\text{i})(\\sqrt{3}t-\\sqrt{5}\\text{i})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting this back into our main expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{t}^{4}+30{t}^{2}+25 & =(3{t}^{2}+5)^{2} \\\\  & =((\\sqrt{3}t+\\sqrt{5}\\text{i})(\\sqrt{3}t-\\sqrt{5}\\text{i}))^{2} \\\\  & =(\\sqrt{3}t+\\sqrt{5}\\text{i})^{2}(\\sqrt{3}t-\\sqrt{5}\\text{i})^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262200 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2684fbd5-5ae7-50ec-bb0e-05dc8c55df80" :knowledge-point/title "Factoring a Sum of Squares"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00c49189-8eb1-5585-975d-2410d1097426" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+6i)(x-6i)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262202} {:answer/id #uuid "1e2f06fd-8c5a-52a5-8f2d-6a112458cd7b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+6)(x-6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262203}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "00c49189-8eb1-5585-975d-2410d1097426" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+6i)(x-6i)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262202} :answer-field/id #uuid "dfc7747f-169a-5595-9235-eada96ab49f7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262201}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f38ce265-3fba-5b1a-82fa-e20bbd612f01" :question/math-academy-id "q-319438" :question/problem "Write ${x}^{2}+36$ in factored form by rewriting the second term of the expression as the square of an imaginary number. Use $i$ to denote the imaginary unit.\n\n${x}^{2}+36=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To factor a sum of squares, we first write it as a difference. Then, we rewrite the second term using ${i}^{2}=-1.$ Finally, we factor as a difference of squares:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+36 & ={x}^{2}-(-36) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-(36{i}^{2}) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-(6i)^{2} \\\\  & =(x+6i)(x-6i)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262204 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c55fcfa4-0050-5a80-a142-29616155797f" :knowledge-point/title "Factoring a Perfect Square Quartic Trinomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ad1d89d9-d173-52a5-856a-509ac9175566" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+i\\sqrt{5})^2(x-i\\sqrt{5})^2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262206} {:answer/id #uuid "06cd9553-b8ee-5272-870f-8c369d280282" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+5i)^2(x-5i)^2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262207}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ad1d89d9-d173-52a5-856a-509ac9175566" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+i\\sqrt{5})^2(x-i\\sqrt{5})^2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262206} :answer-field/id #uuid "2166193f-e9df-5425-a134-4e68c5f74e91" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262205}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "15785172-c630-5198-be0a-963b458e2faa" :question/math-academy-id "q-319471" :question/problem "Write ${x}^{4}+10{x}^{2}+25$ in factored form as $(a+bi)^{2}(a-bi)^{2}.$ Use $i$ to denote the imaginary unit.\n\n${x}^{4}+10{x}^{2}+25=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, let's factor the expression as a perfect square trinomial in ${x}^{2}.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{4}+10{x}^{2}+25 & ={({x}^{2})}^{2}+2\\cdot 5\\cdot ({x}^{2})+{5}^{2} \\\\  & ={({x}^{2}+5)}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to factor the sum of squares in the parentheses. To do this, we first write it as a difference. Then, we rewrite the second term using ${i}^{2}=-1.$ Finally, we factor as a difference of squares.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+5 & ={x}^{2}-(-5) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-(5{i}^{2}) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-{(\\sqrt{5}\\,i)}^{2} \\\\  & =(x+\\sqrt{5}\\,i)(x-\\sqrt{5}\\,i)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting this back into our main expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{4}+10{x}^{2}+25 & ={({x}^{2}+5)}^{2} \\\\  & ={((x+\\sqrt{5}\\,i)(x-\\sqrt{5}\\,i))}^{2} \\\\  & ={(x+\\sqrt{5}\\,i)}^{2}{(x-\\sqrt{5}\\,i)}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262208 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2684fbd5-5ae7-50ec-bb0e-05dc8c55df80" :knowledge-point/title "Factoring a Sum of Squares"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f47578b0-9cd2-541c-87ea-b960c35e1eec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+7i)(x-7i)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262210}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f47578b0-9cd2-541c-87ea-b960c35e1eec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+7i)(x-7i)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262210} :answer-field/id #uuid "536d74f9-e3f5-5fbf-ad8d-df64862ba408" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262209}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3907cfb2-7d8c-510e-95a8-7d00cf19d953" :question/math-academy-id "q-320695" :question/problem "Write ${x}^{2}+49$ in factored form by rewriting the second term of the expression as the square of an imaginary number. Use $i$ to denote the imaginary unit.\n\n${x}^{2}+49=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To factor a sum of squares, we first write it as a difference. Then, we rewrite the second term using ${i}^{2}=-1.$ Finally, we factor as a difference of squares:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+49 & ={x}^{2}-(-49) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-(49{i}^{2}) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-(7i)^{2} \\\\  & =(x+7i)(x-7i)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262211 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2684fbd5-5ae7-50ec-bb0e-05dc8c55df80" :knowledge-point/title "Factoring a Sum of Squares"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "51a4f5f6-c75a-54c3-8dce-74ec3dfea750" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(7r+3i)(7r-3i)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262213}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "51a4f5f6-c75a-54c3-8dce-74ec3dfea750" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(7r+3i)(7r-3i)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262213} :answer-field/id #uuid "577a73dc-66f3-5a4e-b483-92c0980e5793" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262212}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "17ef67bf-515c-5bf1-9ff5-94a7f17bfa40" :question/math-academy-id "q-320731" :question/problem "Write $49{r}^{2}+9$ in factored form by rewriting the second term of the expression as the square of an imaginary number. Use $i$ to denote the imaginary unit.\n\n$49{r}^{2}+9=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To factor a sum of squares, we first write it as a difference. Then, we rewrite the second term using ${i}^{2}=-1.$ Finally, we factor as a difference of squares:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}49{r}^{2}+9 & =49{r}^{2}-(-9) \\\\  & =49{r}^{2}-(9{i}^{2}) \\\\  & =(7r)^{2}-(3i)^{2} \\\\  & =(7r+3i)(7r-3i)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262214 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2684fbd5-5ae7-50ec-bb0e-05dc8c55df80" :knowledge-point/title "Factoring a Sum of Squares"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5ca79658-a738-59a2-a797-8b1efcc0d177" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(m+5n)(m-5n)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262216} {:answer/id #uuid "2807e752-6d66-56d1-bb16-b678cf48f1d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(m+5in)(m-5in)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262217}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2807e752-6d66-56d1-bb16-b678cf48f1d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(m+5in)(m-5in)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262217} :answer-field/id #uuid "59a1f7ff-3dbf-5f13-9586-fac332d511aa" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262215}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3d207584-1bdc-593d-8216-fb56104d4bb9" :question/math-academy-id "q-320739" :question/problem "Write ${m}^{2}+25{n}^{2}$ in factored form by rewriting the second term of the expression as the square of an imaginary number. Use $i$ to denote the imaginary unit.\n\n${m}^{2}+25{n}^{2}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To factor a sum of squares, we first write it as a difference. Then, we rewrite the second term using ${i}^{2}=-1.$ Finally, we factor as a difference of squares.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}+25{n}^{2} & ={m}^{2}-(-25{n}^{2}) \\\\  & ={m}^{2}-(25{i}^{2}{n}^{2}) \\\\  & ={m}^{2}-(5in)^{2} \\\\  & =(m+5in)(m-5in)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262218 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8216c637-fe4d-51fd-b256-0379eb0e7dba" :knowledge-point/title "Factoring a Sum of Higher-Degree Squares"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5a01336f-3aed-541f-bc4b-24849652ca8e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(r^3+2)(r^3-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262220} {:answer/id #uuid "6189679e-cab4-5533-b8d2-07511bcc5006" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(r^3+2i)(r^3-2i)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262221}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6189679e-cab4-5533-b8d2-07511bcc5006" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(r^3+2i)(r^3-2i)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262221} :answer-field/id #uuid "feaadf76-1ae8-5c97-9976-80e4ba3ccda2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262219}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7af5c447-534a-541e-a009-eb560d8f021f" :question/math-academy-id "q-320744" :question/problem "Write ${r}^{6}+4$ in factored form by rewriting the second term of the expression as the square of an imaginary number. Use $i$ to denote the imaginary unit.\n\n${r}^{6}+4=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given expression is a sum of squares, since\n\n$$\n{r}^{6}+4={({r}^{3})}^{2}+{2}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTo factor a sum of squares, we first write it as a difference. Then, we rewrite the second term using ${i}^{2}=-1.$ Finally, we factor as a difference of squares.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{6}+4 & ={r}^{6}-(-4) \\\\  & ={r}^{6}-(4{i}^{2}) \\\\  & ={({r}^{3})}^{2}-{(2i)}^{2} \\\\  & =({r}^{3}+2i)({r}^{3}-2i)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262222 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8216c637-fe4d-51fd-b256-0379eb0e7dba" :knowledge-point/title "Factoring a Sum of Higher-Degree Squares"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7fd477c0-dba7-51cf-957a-97b536ee5ca2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4p^6+im^5)(4p^6-im^5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262224}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7fd477c0-dba7-51cf-957a-97b536ee5ca2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4p^6+im^5)(4p^6-im^5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262224} :answer-field/id #uuid "d4e56128-ce3b-50cd-b4cd-f7f718a2a0bf" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262223}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d788f9e8-a357-5b0b-8ecb-f9a36fd7b3ea" :question/math-academy-id "q-320771" :question/problem "Write $16{p}^{12}+{m}^{10}$ in factored form by rewriting the second term of the expression as the square of an imaginary number. Use $i$ to denote the imaginary unit.\n\n$16{p}^{12}+{m}^{10}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given expression is a sum of squares, since\n\n$$\n16{p}^{12}+{m}^{10}={(4{p}^{6})}^{2}+{({m}^{5})}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTo factor a sum of squares, we first write it as a difference. Then, we rewrite the second term using ${i}^{2}=-1.$ Finally, we factor as a difference of squares.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}16{p}^{12}+{m}^{10} & =16{p}^{12}-(-{m}^{10}) \\\\  & =16{p}^{12}-({i}^{2}{m}^{10}) \\\\  & ={(4{p}^{6})}^{2}-{(i{m}^{5})}^{2} \\\\  & =(4{p}^{6}+i{m}^{5})(4{p}^{6}-i{m}^{5})\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262225 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2de6dbad-0ca5-5a17-a04b-cdfd72d6dab5" :knowledge-point/title "Factoring an Expression Containing a Sum of Squares"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4cfacf4c-3657-5b44-9f8e-064e23dfbf7a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(3c+5i)(3c-5i)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262227}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4cfacf4c-3657-5b44-9f8e-064e23dfbf7a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(3c+5i)(3c-5i)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262227} :answer-field/id #uuid "f09fc79a-bdfc-562e-a8dd-0814dc4d683d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262226}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a5380849-083c-5460-8e52-ea0790b87768" :question/math-academy-id "q-320776" :question/problem "Write $18{c}^{2}+50$ in factored form by rewriting the second term of the expression as the square of an imaginary number. Use $i$ to denote the imaginary unit.\n\n$18{c}^{2}+50=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, let's factor out $2$ from the expression:\n\n$$\n18{c}^{2}+50=2\\,(9{c}^{2}+25).\n$$\n\nNow focus on factoring $(9{c}^{2}+25).$  \n\nTo factor a sum of squares, we first write it as a difference. Then, we rewrite the second term using ${i}^{2}=-1.$ Finally, we factor it as a difference of squares.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{c}^{2}+25 & =9{c}^{2}-(-25) \\\\  & =9{c}^{2}-(25{i}^{2}) \\\\  & =(3c)^{2}-(5i)^{2} \\\\  & =(3c+5i)(3c-5i).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting back, we obtain\n\n$$\n2\\,(9{c}^{2}+25)=2(3c+5i)(3c-5i).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262228 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2de6dbad-0ca5-5a17-a04b-cdfd72d6dab5" :knowledge-point/title "Factoring an Expression Containing a Sum of Squares"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "41815ca1-7809-5f15-8240-4df3805f0208" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3(2t+5i)(2t-5i)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262230} {:answer/id #uuid "754b7ed6-1f1d-5b5b-9fcd-eb46d865566d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3(2t+5)(2t-5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262231}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "41815ca1-7809-5f15-8240-4df3805f0208" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3(2t+5i)(2t-5i)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262230} :answer-field/id #uuid "0d108b09-24e3-5d21-9ff6-5c841d55aeb2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262229}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "04c63495-d11a-5409-8504-cac9a2592426" :question/math-academy-id "q-320780" :question/problem "Write $12{t}^{2}+75$ in factored form by rewriting the second term of the expression as the square of an imaginary number. Use $i$ to denote the imaginary unit.\n\n$12{t}^{2}+75=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, let's factor out $3$ from the expression:\n\n$$\n12{t}^{2}+75=3\\,(4{t}^{2}+25).\n$$\n\nNow focus on factoring $(4{t}^{2}+25).$  \n\nTo factor a sum of squares, we first write it as a difference. Then, we rewrite the second term using ${i}^{2}=-1.$ Finally, we factor it as a difference of squares.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{t}^{2}+25 & =4{t}^{2}-(-25) \\\\  & =4{t}^{2}-(25{i}^{2}) \\\\  & =(2t)^{2}-(5i)^{2} \\\\  & =(2t+5i)(2t-5i).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting back, we obtain\n\n$$\n3\\,(4{t}^{2}+25)=3(2t+5i)(2t-5i).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262232 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2de6dbad-0ca5-5a17-a04b-cdfd72d6dab5" :knowledge-point/title "Factoring an Expression Containing a Sum of Squares"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8ccc25ca-a24e-5ea4-9057-b639c0f711fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2(3s+4i)(3s-4i)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262234}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8ccc25ca-a24e-5ea4-9057-b639c0f711fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2(3s+4i)(3s-4i)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262234} :answer-field/id #uuid "f2f33061-699e-532e-9802-00ece96f7f98" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262233}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ce872820-a659-5d59-b5ee-57498846afc0" :question/math-academy-id "q-320785" :question/problem "Write $-18{s}^{2}-32$ in factored form by rewriting the second term of the expression as the square of an imaginary number. Use $i$ to denote the imaginary unit.\n\n$-18{s}^{2}-32=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, let's factor out $-2$ from the expression:\n\n$$\n-18{s}^{2}-32=-2\\,(9{s}^{2}+16).\n$$\n\nNow focus on factoring $(9{s}^{2}+16).$  \n\nTo factor a sum of squares, we first write it as a difference. Then, we rewrite the second term using ${i}^{2}=-1.$ Finally, we factor it as a difference of squares.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{s}^{2}+16 & =9{s}^{2}-(-16) \\\\  & =9{s}^{2}-(16{i}^{2}) \\\\  & =(3s)^{2}-(4i)^{2} \\\\  & =(3s+4i)(3s-4i).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting back, we obtain\n\n$$\n-2\\,(9{s}^{2}+16)=-2(3s+4i)(3s-4i).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262235 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2de6dbad-0ca5-5a17-a04b-cdfd72d6dab5" :knowledge-point/title "Factoring an Expression Containing a Sum of Squares"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08f180a9-ef25-51ac-9b63-cd1b8a146e07" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t^5(7t^2+6i)(7t^2-6i)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262237} {:answer/id #uuid "7acc6f4c-587a-55f7-951b-de6ef609f23a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t^5(7t^2+6)(7t^2-6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262238}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "08f180a9-ef25-51ac-9b63-cd1b8a146e07" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t^5(7t^2+6i)(7t^2-6i)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262237} :answer-field/id #uuid "b453bf26-4fe6-5175-b1d1-86c390d715f5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262236}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6ff868e4-26d0-57ff-a62e-75e89aef9101" :question/math-academy-id "q-320788" :question/problem "Write $49{t}^{9}+36{t}^{5}$ in factored form by rewriting the second term of the expression as the square of an imaginary number. Use $i$ to denote the imaginary unit.\n\n$49{t}^{9}+36{t}^{5}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, let's factor out ${t}^{5}$ from the expression:\n\n$$\n49{t}^{9}+36{t}^{5}={t}^{5}\\,(49{t}^{4}+36).\n$$\n\nNow focus on factoring $(49{t}^{4}+36).$  \n\nTo factor a sum of squares, we first write it as a difference. Then, we rewrite the second term using ${i}^{2}=-1.$ Finally, we factor it as a difference of squares.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}49{t}^{4}+36 & =49{t}^{4}-(-36) \\\\  & =49{t}^{4}-(36{i}^{2}) \\\\  & =(7{t}^{2})^{2}-(6i)^{2} \\\\  & =(7{t}^{2}+6i)(7{t}^{2}-6i).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting back, we obtain\n\n$$\n{t}^{5}\\,(49{t}^{4}+36)={t}^{5}(7{t}^{2}+6i)(7{t}^{2}-6i).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262239 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c55fcfa4-0050-5a80-a142-29616155797f" :knowledge-point/title "Factoring a Perfect Square Quartic Trinomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f0f3cda6-0426-5ef3-9ecb-780293a71e7d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2x+i)^2(2x-i)^2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262241}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f0f3cda6-0426-5ef3-9ecb-780293a71e7d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2x+i)^2(2x-i)^2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262241} :answer-field/id #uuid "4ba2619c-c113-5f6d-9e5f-6d1217e53f3a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262240}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a780393b-b1a4-5e5d-8b0b-c78216aed36b" :question/math-academy-id "q-320891" :question/problem "Write $16{x}^{4}+8{x}^{2}+1$ in factored form as $(a+bi)^{2}(a-bi)^{2}.$ Use $i$ to denote the imaginary unit.\n\n$16{x}^{4}+8{x}^{2}+1=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, let's factor the expression as a perfect square trinomial in ${b}^{2}.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}16{x}^{4}+8{x}^{2}+1 & ={(4{x}^{2})}^{2}+2\\cdot (4{x}^{2})\\cdot 1+{1}^{2} \\\\  & ={(4{x}^{2}+1)}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to factor the sum of squares in the parentheses. To do this, we first write it as a difference. Then, we rewrite the second term using ${i}^{2}=-1.$ Finally, we factor as a difference of squares.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}+1 & =4{x}^{2}-(-1) \\\\  & =4{x}^{2}-({i}^{2}) \\\\  & ={(2x)}^{2}-{(i)}^{2} \\\\  & =(2x+i)(2x-i)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting this back into our main expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}16{x}^{4}+8{x}^{2}+1 & ={(4{x}^{2}+1)}^{2} \\\\  & ={((2x+i)(2x-i))}^{2} \\\\  & ={(2x+i)}^{2}{(2x-i)}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262242 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c55fcfa4-0050-5a80-a142-29616155797f" :knowledge-point/title "Factoring a Perfect Square Quartic Trinomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e56ff8b8-9576-5051-919f-a12a250b9105" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3y+i)^2(3y-i)^2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262244} {:answer/id #uuid "851007e2-763f-57b2-aa96-add36f217aa7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3y+1)^2(3y-1)^2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262245}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e56ff8b8-9576-5051-919f-a12a250b9105" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3y+i)^2(3y-i)^2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262244} :answer-field/id #uuid "83e671b5-5d29-546b-a844-31729b46cfba" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262243}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "6b680876-de58-5871-8a5a-95c40df18679" :question/math-academy-id "q-367715" :question/problem "Write $81{y}^{4}+18{y}^{2}+1$ in factored form as $(a+bi)^{2}(a-bi)^{2}.$ Use $i$ to denote the imaginary unit.\n\n$81{y}^{4}+18{y}^{2}+1=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, let's factor the expression as a perfect square trinomial in ${y}^{2}.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}81{y}^{4}+18{y}^{2}+1 & ={(9{y}^{2})}^{2}+2\\cdot (9{y}^{2})\\cdot 1+{1}^{2} \\\\  & ={(9{y}^{2}+1)}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to factor the sum of squares in the parentheses. To do this, we first write it as a difference. Then, we rewrite the second term using ${i}^{2}=-1.$ Finally, we factor as a difference of squares.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{y}^{2}+1 & =9{y}^{2}-(-1) \\\\  & =9{y}^{2}-({i}^{2}) \\\\  & ={(3y)}^{2}-{(i)}^{2} \\\\  & =(3y+i)(3y-i)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting this back into our main expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}81{y}^{4}+18{y}^{2}+1 & ={(9{y}^{2}+1)}^{2} \\\\  & ={((3y+i)(3y-i))}^{2} \\\\  & ={(3y+i)}^{2}{(3y-i)}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262246 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c55fcfa4-0050-5a80-a142-29616155797f" :knowledge-point/title "Factoring a Perfect Square Quartic Trinomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "008ec3df-d702-5bf1-acbd-3abbc0b78538" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+5i)^2(x-5i)^2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262248}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "008ec3df-d702-5bf1-acbd-3abbc0b78538" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+5i)^2(x-5i)^2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262248} :answer-field/id #uuid "f54a709a-63f3-5f2c-861f-7391085d7b3e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262247}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "710681dd-b391-58c3-8b1b-c34afdb4e6f7" :question/math-academy-id "q-389797" :question/problem "Write ${x}^{4}+50{x}^{2}+625$ in factored form as $(a+bi)^{2}(a-bi)^{2}.$ Use $i$ to denote the imaginary unit.\n\n${x}^{4}+50{x}^{2}+625=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, let's factor the expression as a perfect square trinomial in ${x}^{2}.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{4}+50{x}^{2}+625 & ={({x}^{2})}^{2}+2\\cdot 25\\cdot ({x}^{2})+{25}^{2} \\\\  & ={({x}^{2}+25)}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to factor the sum of squares in the parentheses. To do this, we first write it as a difference. Then, we rewrite the second term using ${i}^{2}=-1.$ Finally, we factor as a difference of squares.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+25 & ={x}^{2}-(-25) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-(25{i}^{2}) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-{(5i)}^{2} \\\\  & =(x+5i)(x-5i)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting this back into our main expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{4}+50{x}^{2}+625 & ={({x}^{2}+25)}^{2} \\\\  & ={((x+5i)(x-5i))}^{2} \\\\  & ={(x+5i)}^{2}{(x-5i)}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262249 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2de6dbad-0ca5-5a17-a04b-cdfd72d6dab5" :knowledge-point/title "Factoring an Expression Containing a Sum of Squares"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c0da53e0-301e-5b0d-a508-b2b007206ea6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3+pt)(3-pt)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262251} {:answer/id #uuid "eaf812f1-f505-50dc-96f8-00adbe013391" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3+\\text{i}pt)(3-\\text{i}pt)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262252} {:answer/id #uuid "f0417094-366a-59f0-b827-8f7eab085afd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p(3+t)(3-t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262253} {:answer/id #uuid "5e0fa95a-5b40-5dc2-a62b-94ae3bbea427" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p(3+\\text{i}t)(3-\\text{i}t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262254} {:answer/id #uuid "aac82f08-e874-5ec0-84c5-449c609571e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p(3+\\text{i}pt)(3-\\text{i}pt)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262255}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5e0fa95a-5b40-5dc2-a62b-94ae3bbea427" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p(3+\\text{i}t)(3-\\text{i}t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262254} :answer-field/id #uuid "279ef482-f109-5531-b653-7309f92e0aa2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262250}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "90a0851c-25fb-565a-8ffc-02325f1722fc" :question/math-academy-id "q-46443" :question/problem "The expression $9p+p{t}^{2}$ is equivalent to" :question/worked-solution "First, let's factor $p$ from the expression. We have\n\n$$\n9p+p{t}^{2}=p(9+{t}^{2}).\n$$\n\nLet's now focus on factoring the expression $(9+{t}^{2}).$\n\nTo factor a sum of squares, we first write it as a difference. Then, we rewrite the second term using ${\\text{i}}^{2}=-1.$ Finally, we factor as a difference of squares.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9+{t}^{2} & =9-(-{t}^{2}) \\\\  & =9-({\\text{i}}^{2}{t}^{2}) \\\\  & ={3}^{2}-(\\text{i}t)^{2} \\\\  & =(3+\\text{i}t)(3-\\text{i}t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting this back into our main expression, we get\n\n$$\np(9+{t}^{2})=p(3+\\text{i}t)(3-\\text{i}t).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262256 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2684fbd5-5ae7-50ec-bb0e-05dc8c55df80" :knowledge-point/title "Factoring a Sum of Squares"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e0f5a3dc-e58a-5d8a-ba08-7777ce18588a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3w+4)(3w-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262258} {:answer/id #uuid "74084b6f-077b-54ad-9639-90d2a99cb625" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3w+16\\text{i})(3w-16\\text{i})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262259} {:answer/id #uuid "e289419f-cf51-52d6-96ff-9c22bf2f427c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9(w+4\\text{i})(w-4\\text{i})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262260} {:answer/id #uuid "c5b17f07-f00d-5dd2-be57-b7d6b0875667" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9(w+4)(w-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262261} {:answer/id #uuid "7b0f58ec-6bc0-5b1f-9388-97502db6802c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3w+4\\text{i})(3w-4\\text{i})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262262}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7b0f58ec-6bc0-5b1f-9388-97502db6802c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3w+4\\text{i})(3w-4\\text{i})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262262} :answer-field/id #uuid "4c805480-1cbc-5592-a5a4-93396da48803" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262257}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d875c050-5959-5bc2-9f2b-514498015ab1" :question/math-academy-id "q-46491" :question/problem "Factor $9{w}^{2}+16.$" :question/worked-solution "To factor a sum of squares, we first write it as a difference. Then, we rewrite the second term using ${\\text{i}}^{2}=-1.$ Finally, we factor as a difference of squares.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{w}^{2}+16 & =9{w}^{2}-(-16) \\\\  & =9{w}^{2}-(16{\\text{i}}^{2}) \\\\  & =(3w)^{2}-{(4\\text{i})}^{2} \\\\  & =(3w+4\\text{i})(3w-4\\text{i}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262263 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8216c637-fe4d-51fd-b256-0379eb0e7dba" :knowledge-point/title "Factoring a Sum of Higher-Degree Squares"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "91fa2cb5-3f1b-5d19-b814-3be2a0645b82" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3m+2\\text{i}{n}^{2})(3m-2\\text{i}{n}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262265} {:answer/id #uuid "7f5e8f37-7284-584e-a045-a0a3aadf21bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9\\,({m}^{2}+\\text{i}{n}^{5})({m}^{2}-\\text{i}{n}^{5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262266} {:answer/id #uuid "00b059d6-8f92-5f6a-a773-db97bef047a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3m+2{n}^{2})(3m-2{n}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262267} {:answer/id #uuid "c17e32af-77b2-5562-baa4-d1094fb33886" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9\\,({m}^{2}+2{n}^{5})({m}^{2}-2{n}^{5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262268} {:answer/id #uuid "6151af81-45c5-5aa4-9d98-2e201f181e5c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3{m}^{2}+2\\text{i}{n}^{5})(3{m}^{2}-2\\text{i}{n}^{5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262269}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6151af81-45c5-5aa4-9d98-2e201f181e5c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3{m}^{2}+2\\text{i}{n}^{5})(3{m}^{2}-2\\text{i}{n}^{5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262269} :answer-field/id #uuid "fd924dce-3b06-5ec5-a586-aba7a459b1d9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262264}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3836c1ea-4956-533e-b395-a4a0ae49ee76" :question/math-academy-id "q-46502" :question/problem "$9{m}^{4}+4{n}^{10}=$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given expression is a sum of squares, since\n\n$$\n9{m}^{4}+4{n}^{10}={(3{m}^{2})}^{2}+{(2{n}^{5})}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTo factor a sum of squares, we first write it as a difference. Then, we rewrite the second term using ${\\text{i}}^{2}=-1.$ Finally, we factor as a difference of squares.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{m}^{4}+4{n}^{10} & =9{m}^{4}-(-4{n}^{10}) \\\\  & =9{m}^{4}-(4{\\text{i}}^{2}{n}^{10}) \\\\  & ={(3{m}^{2})}^{2}-{(2\\text{i}{n}^{5})}^{2} \\\\  & =(3{m}^{2}+2\\text{i}{n}^{5})(3{m}^{2}-2\\text{i}{n}^{5})\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262270 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8216c637-fe4d-51fd-b256-0379eb0e7dba" :knowledge-point/title "Factoring a Sum of Higher-Degree Squares"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "70115adc-d19d-52d2-bce1-1c6fd1e66d0a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{({x}^{2}-3\\text{i})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262272} {:answer/id #uuid "0cb5000c-1e8c-55a2-9990-20266613c4d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({x}^{2}+3)({x}^{2}-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262273} {:answer/id #uuid "69bed38c-8082-55ba-aed2-b0a8e60186a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{({x}^{2}-3)}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262274} {:answer/id #uuid "ebb215aa-417a-5920-8a59-5fce4c5deeb1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({x}^{2}+3\\text{i})({x}^{2}-3\\text{i})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262275} {:answer/id #uuid "3178792c-3f8b-5241-9f57-9b0dda5615eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{({x}^{2}+3\\text{i})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262276}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ebb215aa-417a-5920-8a59-5fce4c5deeb1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({x}^{2}+3\\text{i})({x}^{2}-3\\text{i})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262275} :answer-field/id #uuid "24c48979-d367-54b0-8c18-1189df6e545f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262271}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4346369e-cdee-51e0-b640-ab0ca22e5f6f" :question/math-academy-id "q-87324" :question/problem "Factor ${x}^{4}+9.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given expression is a sum of squares, since\n\n$$\n{x}^{4}+9={({x}^{2})}^{2}+{3}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTo factor a sum of squares, we first write it as a difference. Then, we rewrite the second term using ${\\text{i}}^{2}=-1.$ Finally, we factor as a difference of squares.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{4}+9 & ={x}^{4}-(-9) \\\\  & ={x}^{4}-(9{\\text{i}}^{2}) \\\\  & ={({x}^{2})}^{2}-{(3\\text{i})}^{2} \\\\  & =({x}^{2}+3\\text{i})({x}^{2}-3\\text{i})\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
